      "math_academy_id": "q-155053",
      "sequence_position": 8,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/a31a793df705b6cbe8dddb648ca6a0d2ebb6f4eb724ff8b99682f48cafb072a0.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams above shows three collinear points?",
      "worked_solution": "Three (or more) points are collinear if a straight line passes through all points.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our diagrams in turn.\n\n- Diagram I shows three points that are *not* collinear. We can't draw a straight line that passes through all three points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/aad13ab456bc61c90522725ddb60f481c6138a9e53a50136d691ddd344349cf1.png)\n\n- Diagram II shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/143e831321a485a0e57b706c6df89273bd92b3134d691d1ef41a67b7f7076481.png)\n\n- Diagram III shows three collinear points. There is a straight line that passes through all three of these points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14219315/assets/2c361eea2a598de063dc62375716ca5bdb7b0a06d4c30cf9a5c420cd9cc48e4b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
